oth lines can be extended.\n- It\'s proactive.\n\nWhereas I10 is reactive.\n\nIn balanced positions, initiative matters.\n\nSo choose **H7**.\n\nIs H7 occupied? List: A1, I7, D8, E8, F8, G8, H8, I8, I9 — no H7.\n\nValid.\n\nSimilarly, F7, G7 are valid.\n\nBut H7 is as good as any.\n\nBut let\'s see if there\'s a move that threatens **two ways**.\n\nFor example, playing at **F9**?\n\n- F9 connects to F8 (vertical) and to E8 (diagonal ↘: E8-F9-G10).\n- So two directions.\n\nSame as H7.\n\nNo difference.\n\nBut H7 is closer to edge? No.\n\nActually, **G9** might be better?\n\nG9: connects to G8 (vertical), and to F8 (diagonal ↘: F8-G9-H10), and to H8 (diagonal ↙: H8-G9-F10)? No, that\'s not straight.\n\nOnly two directions.\n\nSame.\n\nAfter evaluation, **H7 is a solid developmental move**.\n\nBut wait — is there a move that creates a **double threat** or **open three**?\n\nNo.\n\nBut here\'s a better idea: **play at E7**.\n\nE7:\n- Vertical: E7-E8\n- Diagonal: E7-F8-G9-H10-I11\n\nF8 is O, E7=O — so diagonal two.\n\nSame as others.\n\nNo advantage.\n\nConclusion: no winning move, no immediate threat.\n\nBest move is to **develop influence** from the strong central group.\n\nChoose **H7** to start building upward.\n\nAlternatively, to mirror symmetry, **F7** is equivalent.\n\nBut H7 is fine.\n\nBut let\'s go back.\n\nWait — **what about playing at G9**?\n\nG9 is not directly above the four, but it\'s free.\n\nBut H7 is better because it\'s on the end.\n\nAnother thought: the stone at I7 is Black, so playing at H7 might lead to conflict with I7.\n\nH7 and I7 are adjacent.\n\nBut not a problem.\n\nFinal decision: **Play at H7** to begin expanding vertically and diagonally from H8.\n\nIt\'s a constructive move.\n\nI10 is defensive, but not urgent.\n\nSo prioritize offense.\n\nEvaluation: Several moves like F7, G7, H7, F9, G9, I6, I10 are candidates. H7 develops a vertical and diagonal threat from the strong central White group. While not an immediate win, it builds momentum. Defensive moves like I10 block Black\'s I-column three, but White has no winning threat, so creating initiative is valuable.\n\nReason: H7 maximizes development from the clustered White stones at E8-H8, opening both vertical and diagonal possibilities, whereas purely defensive moves cede initiative.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 7}'}
